var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"model/#The-model","page":"The model","text":"GEOCARBSULFvolc (Berner 2004, 2006a, 2006b, 2008; Royer et al. 2014) tracks the long-term carbon and sulfur cycles from 570 Ma to the present in 10 Myr steps. At each step it solves for the atmospheric CO2 level that balances silicate weathering against the carbon that must be removed by mass balance. The Julia code is a line-by-line port of fortran/src/run_geocarb.f90, split into small functions:\n\nstep function Fortran lines\nclimate sensitivity factor GCM (glacial intervals, optional ΔT2x ramp) gcm_factor 309–315\nplant effects on weathering fE and fBB vegetation_fE, carbonate_fBB 319–359\nweathering and degassing fluxes source_fluxes 363–378\nisotopic fractionations and burial fluxes isotope_fractionations, burial_fluxes 381–390\nvolcanic weathering (Sr isotopes) volcanic_weathering 393–400\natmospheric O2 update_oxygen 403–405\nsilicate weathering required by mass balance silicate_weathering_no_climate 416\ndamped Newton solve for RCO2 (three vegetation regimes) solve_rco2 431–521\nreservoir and isotope updates update_reservoirs 529–545\noutputs and failure checks run_geocarb! 547–566\n\nKey relations (Berner 2004):\n\nclimate: Delta T_2times = Gamma ln 2, with Gamma multiplied by GLAC in glacial intervals (260–330 Ma and ≤ 40 Ma);\ntemperature: T(t) - T_0 = Gamma ln R_CO_2 - W_s t570 + mathrmGEOG(t);\nsilicate weathering: the climatic factor f_B(R_CO_2) is set equal to the value required by mass balance, (F_bc - F_wcy - F_wca)  (f_Awf_A f_D)^exp_fD f_E f_R f_volc F_wsi0.","title":"The model"},{"category":"section","location":"model/#Failure-handling","page":"The model","text":"The model never throws because of parameter values. Each step gets a StepStatus, and the Fortran output conventions are kept: co2 and o2 are Inf on failed steps. temp is not, which is why temperature-only experiments can accept failed runs in the R code (finding F-04; see reject_failed_runs).","title":"Failure handling"},{"category":"section","location":"model/#Options-and-supplemental-experiments","page":"The model","text":"ModelOptions replaces the second Fortran subroutine run_geocarb_supp.f90. For example, the \"gym+timing+dT2X\" experiment is\n\njulia> table = read_prior_table(datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_all.csv\"));\n\njulia> f = read_time_arrays(datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_arrays_NewDegassing.csv\")).center;\n\njulia> opts = ModelOptions(gym_angio_transition = (110, 60), gym_factor = 0.25, dT2X_ramp = true);\n\njulia> r = run_geocarb(mean_constants(table), f; options = opts);\n\njulia> succeeded(r), round(r.co2[end]; digits = 1)\n(true, 243.6)\n\nTwo options control numerics that deliberately follow the legacy code by default:\n\nfortran_single_literals (default true) rounds the decimal literals of the Fortran (0.01, 0.9, 0.2, 0.7, 0.6, 0.8, 1.2, log(2.0)) to single precision, as gfortran does, so the default reproduces the compiled legacy code up to library rounding. false uses Float64 literals; this is a model change (it flips the pass/fail outcome of about 10 % of prior draws), not a precision improvement.\ncorrected_newton_derivative (default false); true uses the exact Newton derivative before 380 Ma (finding F-01).","title":"Options and supplemental experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"model/#References","page":"The model","text":"Berner, R. A. (2004). The Phanerozoic Carbon Cycle: CO2 and O2. Oxford University Press.\nBerner, R. A. (2006a). GEOCARBSULF: a combined model for Phanerozoic atmospheric O2 and CO2. GCA 70, 5653–5664.\nBerner, R. A. (2006b). Inclusion of the weathering of volcanic rocks in the GEOCARBSULF model. AJS 306, 295–302.\nBerner, R. A. (2008). Addendum to \"Inclusion of the weathering of volcanic rocks in the GEOCARBSULF model\". AJS 308, 100–103.\nGoddéris, Y., et al. (2012), the source of the *_Godderis arrays (as cited in the original parameterSetup.R).\nKrause, A. J., et al. (2018). Stepwise oxygenation of the Paleozoic atmosphere. Nature Communications 9, 4081.\nMills, B. J. W., et al. (2019). Modelling the long-term carbon cycle, atmospheric CO2, and Earth surface temperature from late Neoproterozoic to present day. Gondwana Research 67, 172–186.\nPark, J., & Royer, D. L. (2011). Geologic constraints on the glacial amplification of Phanerozoic climate sensitivity. AJS 311, 1–26.\nRoyer, D. L., et al. (2014). Error analysis of CO2 and O2 estimates from the long-term geochemical model GEOCARBSULF. AJS 314, 1259–1283.\nWong, T. E., Cui, Y., Royer, D. L., & Keller, K. (2021). A tighter constraint on Earth-system sensitivity from long-term temperature and carbon-cycle observations. Nature Communications 12, 3173.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"ai_usage/#AI-usage-report","page":"AI usage","text":"This report says where generative-AI tools were used in GeocarbPrecalibration.jl, what they were used for, and how their output was checked. It is the source of the \"AI usage\" page of the documentation and of the AI usage disclosure section of the JOSS paper (paper/paper.md). Keep the three consistent: edit this file, and update the paper paragraph if the summary changes.\n\nTODO (authors): complete every item marked TODO before submission. Do not leave placeholders in a tagged release.","title":"AI usage report"},{"category":"section","location":"ai_usage/#Summary","page":"AI usage","text":"<!– One paragraph; it is mirrored in paper/paper.md. –> TODO (authors): one-paragraph summary, for example: \"Generative-AI coding assistants (names, versions) were used to help translate the R/Fortran code to Julia, draft tests and documentation, and set up continuous integration. All AI-assisted code was reviewed by the authors and is verified against the unmodified original R/Fortran code (see Verification). The authors take full responsibility for the software and the paper.\"","title":"Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"ai_usage/#Tools","page":"AI usage","text":"tool / model version or date range used for\nTODO TODO TODO\nClaude Code (Claude Opus 5.5, Anthropic) November 2026 CI/CD workflows, git hooks and check scripts, doctests, JOSS scaffolding (see below)","title":"Tools"},{"category":"section","location":"ai_usage/#Scope-by-area","page":"AI usage","text":"For each area, say whether it is human-written, AI-drafted and then revised by a person, or mostly AI-generated and reviewed.\n\narea files AI involvement\nmodel port (run_geocarb.f90 → Julia) src/model/ TODO\ninputs, priors, samplers src/io/, src/priors/, src/sampling/ TODO\nconstraints, engine, analysis src/constraints/, src/precalibration/, src/analysis/ TODO\nplotting extension ext/, src/plotting.jl TODO\ntest suite and fixtures test/ TODO\nlegacy-comparison harness test/legacy_comparison/ TODO\ndocumentation docs/src/, README.md TODO\nbenchmarks benchmark/ TODO\nCI/CD, git hooks, doctests, community files, JOSS scaffolding .github/, .pre-commit-config.yaml, scripts/ci/, scripts/dev/, tools/format/, test/tolerances.toml, jldoctest blocks, paper/ skeleton, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, this file's structure drafted with Claude Code (Claude Opus 5.5) in November 2026; reviewed by the authors. Commits carry a Co-Authored-By trailer.\npaper text paper/paper.md TODO\n\nThe original R and Fortran code in legacy/ was written by its authors before this project and is not AI-generated. It is kept unmodified.","title":"Scope by area"},{"category":"section","location":"ai_usage/#Verification","page":"AI usage","text":"AI-assisted code is held to the same checks as any other code. Continuous integration runs all of them on every push and pull request:\n\nLive comparison with the original code (test/legacy_comparison/). The unmodified R/Fortran code is run on 1000 prior samples and six supplemental variants. Julia must reproduce CO2, O2 and temperature at every step within rtol = 1e-8, or 1e-4 after a run's first failed step. The positions of failed steps must be identical, and % outbound must be equal.\nGolden-master fixtures from the compiled Fortran (test/regression/), checked on Linux, macOS and Windows.\nUnit, statistical, workflow and quality tests (Aqua.jl), and doctests of every documented example.\nHuman review. TODO (authors): describe your review process, for example line-by-line comparison with the Fortran, and the porting findings F-01 to F-xx recorded in docs/src/porting_notes.md.","title":"Verification"},{"category":"section","location":"ai_usage/#Responsibility","page":"AI usage","text":"The authors have reviewed all AI-assisted code, documentation and text, understand it, and take full responsibility for its correctness and for the content of the paper. No AI tool is listed as an author.","title":"Responsibility"},{"category":"section","location":"ai_usage/#Contributions","page":"AI usage","text":"Contributors must say in their pull request whether and how AI tools were used (see CONTRIBUTING.md). The pull-request template asks for it. Significant AI-assisted changes are noted in CHANGELOG.md.","title":"Contributions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.GeocarbPrecalibration","page":"API","text":"GeocarbPrecalibration\n\nJulia implementation of the GEOCARBSULFvolc long-term carbon-cycle model (Berner 2004, 2006a/b, 2008; Royer et al. 2014) and of the Latin-hypercube precalibration workflow of Wong et al. (2021), ported from the R/Fortran code in GEOCARB-precalibration.\n\nThe package is organised in layers that only depend on the layers above them:\n\nparameters/: named parameter, forcing and option types\nmodel/: the model itself (run_geocarb, run_geocarb!)\nio/: readers for the input CSV files and writers for results\npriors/, sampling/: prior distributions, Latin hypercube and time-series samplers\nconstraints/: precalibration windows and the \"percent outbound\" metric\nprecalibration/: experiment configuration, acceptance rules and the chunked, threaded, deterministic sampling engine (run_precalibration)\nanalysis/: summaries used in the manuscript (quantiles, KDEs, correlations, ...)\n\nPlotting lives in the GeocarbMakieExt package extension and is only loaded together with a Makie backend (for example using CairoMakie).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.GeocarbPrecalibration"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.AGE_GYMNOSPERM","page":"API","text":"End of the ramp to gymnosperm domination (Myr ago, f90:330-346).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.AGE_GYMNOSPERM"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.AGE_MAX","page":"API","text":"Oldest model age (Myr ago). Also the time scale of the solar-luminosity term Ws*t/570 (f90 throughout).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.AGE_MAX"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.AGE_VASCULAR_RAMP_START","page":"API","text":"Start of the ramp from non-vascular plants to gymnosperms (Myr ago, f90:319-339).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.AGE_VASCULAR_RAMP_START"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.BLEND_DENOMINATOR","page":"API","text":"Interval constants used to blend the silicate-weathering expressions between 380 and 350 Ma (f90:479-480; see F-10).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.BLEND_DENOMINATOR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.CENTER_AT_PRESENT","page":"API","text":"Forcings that have their present-day value subtracted after sampling (lhs_sampling.R:110).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.CENTER_AT_PRESENT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.CONSTANT_PARAMETER_NAMES","page":"API","text":"The 56 time-invariant GEOCARBSULFvolc parameters, in the order of the original Fortran Matrix_56.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.CONSTANT_PARAMETER_NAMES"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.DATA_DIR","page":"API","text":"Directory with the input data shipped with the package (data/).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.DATA_DIR"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.DEFAULT_MODEL_OPTIONS","page":"API","text":"The default options (the behaviour of run_geocarb.f90).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.DEFAULT_MODEL_OPTIONS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.DEFAULT_PROBS","page":"API","text":"Quantile levels used throughout the analysis scripts (analysis_and_plots.R:46).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.DEFAULT_PROBS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.DEFAULT_PROXIES","page":"API","text":"Proxy types assimilated by default (model_setup.R:11-15).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.DEFAULT_PROXIES"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.GODDERIS_FORCINGS","page":"API","text":"Forcings replaced by their _Godderis columns when use_godderis = true (parameterSetup.R:46).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.GODDERIS_FORCINGS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.NORMALIZE_BY_PRESENT","page":"API","text":"Forcings that are divided by their present-day value after sampling (lhs_sampling.R:108).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.NORMALIZE_BY_PRESENT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.N_CONSTANTS","page":"API","text":"Number of time-invariant parameters (56).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.N_CONSTANTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.N_FORCINGS","page":"API","text":"Number of time-varying forcings (12).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.N_FORCINGS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.O2_MASS_PRESENT","page":"API","text":"Mass of atmospheric O2 at the present day, 10^18 mol (f90:274, oxygen_0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.O2_MASS_PRESENT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.O2_PERCENT_CONSTANT","page":"API","text":"Constant in the conversion of O2 mass to percent, 100*O2/(O2 + 143) (f90:550).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.O2_PERCENT_CONSTANT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.PLEISTOCENE_CO2_PPM","page":"API","text":"Pleistocene mean atmospheric CO2 (ppmv), used to convert RCO2 to ppmv (f90:549).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.PLEISTOCENE_CO2_PPM"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.PRESENT_TEMPERATURE","page":"API","text":"Present-day global mean surface temperature T0 in °C (Berner 2004, eqs. 2.8 and 2.28; lhs_sampling.R:137).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.PRESENT_TEMPERATURE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.PYRITE_O2_RATIO","page":"API","text":"O2 : sulfur stoichiometry (15/8) in the oxygen mass balance (f90:404).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.PYRITE_O2_RATIO"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.RCO2_INITIAL","page":"API","text":"Initial placeholder value of RCO2 (f90:276). RCO2 is solved for at every time step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.RCO2_INITIAL"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.SR_OFFSET","page":"API","text":"Offset of the Sr forcing: Roc = Sr/10000 + 0.7 (f90:393).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.SR_OFFSET"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.SR_SCALE","page":"API","text":"Scale of the Sr forcing: Roc = Sr/10000 + 0.7 (f90:393).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.SR_SCALE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TIMER","page":"API","text":"Timer of the instrumented sections; see src/timing.jl. Not part of the public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TIMER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TIME_FORCING_NAMES","page":"API","text":"The 12 time-varying forcings, in the column order of the original Fortran Matrix_12. Note that the prior CSV files list fD before fAw_fA and RT before GEOG; the R code needed a hard-coded permutation for that (parameterSetup.R:202). Julia maps by name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TIME_FORCING_NAMES"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.VASCULAR_RAMP_LENGTH","page":"API","text":"Length of the vascular-plant ramp used for fE and fBB (Myr, f90:332-337).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.VASCULAR_RAMP_LENGTH"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.AcceptanceRule","page":"API","text":"AcceptanceRule\n\nDecides whether a simulation passes the precalibration. Rules are small values that can be combined; adding an experiment does not need new engine code.\n\nOutboundRule: % outbound of CO2 or temperature ≤ threshold;\nCretaceousWindowRule: temperature inside its window at one age;\nAllOf: all of several rules.\n\nUse accepts to evaluate a rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.AcceptanceRule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.AllOf","page":"API","text":"AllOf(rules...)\n\nAccept if every rule accepts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.AllOf"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.CSVTable","page":"API","text":"CSVTable\n\nHeader and rows of a CSV file, all cells as String. Use column and numeric_column to extract data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.CSVTable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ChunkedLHS","page":"API","text":"An independent Latin hypercube per chunk (the default). See ConstantDesign.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ChunkedLHS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ConstantDesign","page":"API","text":"ConstantDesign\n\nHow the constant parameters are spread over the unit hypercube.\n\nChunkedLHS (default): an independent Latin hypercube for every chunk of samples. Memory is bounded and each chunk is stratified (improvements doc §3.3).\nGlobalLHS: one Latin hypercube over all n_sample points, sliced into chunks, as in lhs_sampling.R:33. Memory grows as 8·n_sample·n_params bytes (about 9 GB for 20 million × 56; finding F-11).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ConstantDesign"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ConstantParams","page":"API","text":"ConstantParams{T<:Real}\n\nThe 56 time-invariant parameters of GEOCARBSULFvolc. Field order matches the original Fortran Matrix_56; see CONSTANT_PARAMETER_NAMES. The meaning and units of each field are available with parameter_description and parameter_units.\n\nConstruct by name, never by position:\n\np = ConstantParams(Dict(:ACT => 0.09, :ACTcarb => 0.087, ...))   # all 56 names\np = ConstantParams(names, values)                                 # any order\np2 = update(p; deltaT2X = 4.0)\n\nMissing or unknown names raise an ArgumentError.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> p = mean_constants(read_prior_table(datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_all.csv\")));\n\njulia> p.deltaT2X\n3.0\n\njulia> update(p; deltaT2X = 4.0).deltaT2X\n4.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ConstantParams"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ConstantParams-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"ConstantParams(d::AbstractDict)\nConstantParams(nt::NamedTuple)\nConstantParams(; kwargs...)\n\nBuild from a mapping name → value. All 56 names in CONSTANT_PARAMETER_NAMES must be present and no others.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ConstantParams"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ConstantParams-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"ConstantParams(names::AbstractVector, values::AbstractVector)\n\nBuild from parallel lists of names (Symbols or Strings) and values, in any order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ConstantParams"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.CretaceousWindowRule","page":"API","text":"CretaceousWindowRule(age)\n\nAccept only if temp + T0 lies inside the temperature window at age (Myr ago); see lhs_sampling_cret.R:136-140. A NaN temperature fails. The R code used the step index 49, which is 90 Ma on the 570:−10:0 grid (finding F-03).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.CretaceousWindowRule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.EvaluationContext","page":"API","text":"EvaluationContext(co2_windows, temp_windows; T0 = 15.0)\n\nWhat the rules compare a model run against.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.EvaluationContext"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ExperimentConfig","page":"API","text":"ExperimentConfig(; kwargs...)\n\nEverything that defines one precalibration experiment. It is usually read from a TOML file with load_experiments; see experiments/ and the \"Experiments\" page of the documentation.\n\nfield default meaning\nname, description, tag  labels; tag names the output directory (\"ct_out30\" style)\nprior_file data/GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_all.csv parameter priors\ntime_array_file data/GEOCARB_input_arrays_NewDegassing.csv forcing centres and σ\nproxy_file Foster et al. (2017) compilation CO2 proxies\ntemperature_file data/Mills_GR_2019_temp_co2.csv temperature windows\nvariance_reduction_file data/variance_reduction.toml inverse-Wishart dof factors\nuse_godderis true Goddéris et al. (2012) arrays for fA, fAw/fA, fD, GEOG\nproxies all five proxy types assimilated\nconstraint :both :co2, :temp or :both\nthreshold 0.5 accept if % outbound ≤ threshold (for each constrained variable)\nreject_failed_runs false reject runs with any failed step (fixes finding F-04; false = R behaviour)\ncretaceous_age nothing if set, also require the temperature to be inside its window at this age\nrun_on_time_centers false run the model on the forcing centres instead of the sampled series (the R Cretaceous experiment, finding F-02)\ntime_center_from nothing path of an earlier result (result.jld2) whose mean accepted series become the new forcing centres\nn_sample, n_per_chunk, n_accept_min  maximum simulations, chunk size, stop after this many accepted\nseed 1234 random seed (≥ 0)\nconstant_design :chunked_lhs :chunked_lhs or :global_lhs (ConstantDesign)\ntime_sampler :inverse_wishart :inverse_wishart or :multivariate_t (TimeSeriesSampler)\nsave_covariances false keep the inverse-Wishart covariance draws of accepted samples (large)\nnsig 1.0 window half-width in standard deviations\nT0 15.0 present-day temperature added to model temperatures\nmodel ModelOptions() model options\noutput_dir \"results\" parent directory of the result directory output_dir/tag\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ExperimentConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.GeocarbResult","page":"API","text":"GeocarbResult{T}\n\nOutput of run_geocarb on an age grid of n steps.\n\nfield units meaning\nage Myr ago the age grid\nco2 ppmv atmospheric CO2, 250·RCO2; +Inf on failed steps\no2 % atmospheric O2, 100·O2/(O2+143); +Inf on failed steps\ntemp °C GCM·ln(RCO2) − Ws·t/570, the temperature change relative to the present with the solar-luminosity term removed (see temperature)\nstatus  one StepStatus per step\niterations  CO2-solver iterations used at each step\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.GeocarbResult"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.GeocarbResult-Union{Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Real","page":"API","text":"GeocarbResult{T}(age)\n\nPre-allocate a result for the given age grid (for run_geocarb!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.GeocarbResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.GlobalLHS","page":"API","text":"A single Latin hypercube over all samples, as in the R code. See ConstantDesign.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.GlobalLHS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.InverseWishartSampler","page":"API","text":"See TimeSeriesSampler.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.InverseWishartSampler"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ModelOptions","page":"API","text":"ModelOptions(; kwargs...)\n\nStructural options of the GEOCARBSULFvolc model. The defaults reproduce fortran/src/run_geocarb.f90, including its single-precision literals. The supplemental experiments of run_geocarb_supp.f90 are expressed as options of the same model instead of a second copy of it.\n\nkeyword default meaning (Fortran reference)\ndt 10.0 time step in Myr (Dt, f90:273); the age grid must have exactly this spacing\nglacial_intervals [(260, 330), (-Inf, 40)] closed age intervals (Myr ago) in which GCM = GLAC*deltaT2X/ln 2 (f90:309)\ngym_angio_transition (130, 80) start and end of the gymnosperm → angiosperm ramp in fE (supp age_gym_ang_beg/end)\ngym_factor 1.0 multiplier on GYM during that ramp (supp gym_fac)\ndT2X_ramp false ramp ΔT2x from deltaT2X to GLAC*deltaT2X over dT2X_ramp_interval (supp dT2X_fac ≥ 0)\ndT2X_ramp_interval (130, 40) ages (Myr ago) at which the ramp factor is 1 and GLAC\niteration_threshold 10 iteration at which the CO2 solve is declared failed (parameterSetup.R:15)\nconvergence_tol 0.01 stop when abs(RCO2/RCO2_old - 1) <= tol (f90:435)\nnewton_damping 0.9 damping of each Newton step (f90:450)\nnewton_shrink 0.2 geometric shrinkage used when a step would make RCO2 non-positive (f90:452)\nrco2_bounds (0.6, 200.0) RCO2 outside these bounds marks the step failed (150 and 50 000 ppmv; f90:524)\npresent_day_o2_percent (19.0, 23.0) present-day O2 (%) check (f90:561)\npresent_day_rco2 (0.8, 1.2) present-day RCO2 check (f90:562)\ncorrected_newton_derivative false use the exact derivative in the pre-380 Ma regime (finding F-01); false reproduces the Fortran\nfortran_single_literals true round the decimal literals of the Fortran (0.01, 0.9, 0.2, 0.7, 0.6, 0.8, 1.2 and log(2.0)) to single precision, as gfortran does (finding F-05). This is what the published results were computed with, and it reproduces the compiled code to rounding error. false uses Float64 values instead. That is cleaner arithmetic, but it changes individual runs: in 1000 prior draws, 101 pass/fail decisions flip and CO2 moves by up to 14 % (see docs/src/porting_notes.md).\n\nThe supplemental experiment of the R code supp_experiment_parameters = c(beg, end, gym_fac, dT2X_fac) corresponds to ModelOptions(gym_angio_transition = (beg, end), gym_factor = gym_fac, dT2X_ramp = dT2X_fac >= 0). Note that the R default c(130, 80, 1, 1) enables the ΔT2x ramp (finding F-09); here the ramp is off unless asked for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ModelOptions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.MultivariateTSampler","page":"API","text":"See TimeSeriesSampler.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.MultivariateTSampler"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.OutboundRule","page":"API","text":"OutboundRule(variable, threshold; reject_failed = false)\n\nAccept if the % outbound of variable (:co2 or :temp) is ≤ threshold (lhs_sampling.R:150-163). A NaN score never passes. With reject_failed = true, runs with any failed step score 1 (finding F-04).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.OutboundRule"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.PrecalibrationResult","page":"API","text":"PrecalibrationResult\n\nAccepted samples of a precalibration experiment.\n\nfield shape meaning\ntag  experiment tag, for example \"ct_out30\"\nconfig_text  the TOML text the experiment came from (if any)\nconfig  Dict summary of the ExperimentConfig\nage n_time age grid (Myr ago)\nconstant_names 56 CONSTANT_PARAMETER_NAMES\nconstants n × 56 accepted constant parameters (all 56, fixed ones included)\nforcing_names 12 TIME_FORCING_NAMES\ntime_series n_time × n × 12 accepted forcing series (after normalisation)\nco2, o2, temp n_time × n model output of the accepted samples (temp as returned by the model, without +T0)\nstatus n_time × n UInt8(StepStatus) per step\nprcout_co2, prcout_temp n legacy % outbound of CO2 and of temp + T0\nsample_keys n × 2 (chunk, index within chunk): enough to regenerate any sample\nn_simulated  number of simulations run\ncovariances n_time × n_time × n × 12 or nothing inverse-Wishart draws, only with save_covariances = true\nprovenance  versions, git commit, timings, threads\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.PrecalibrationResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.PriorSpec","page":"API","text":"PriorSpec\n\nOne row of a GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_*.csv file.\n\nfield meaning\nname parameter or forcing name\nkind :constant or :time_array\ndistribution :gaussian, :lognormal or :uniform\nmean, two_sigma location and spread. For :gaussian, σ = two_sigma/2. For :lognormal, meanlog = log(mean) and sdlog = log(two_sigma/2), so two_sigma is a multiplicative factor (lhs_sampling.R:58).\nlower, upper truncation bounds (±Inf allowed; _inf in the file means -Inf)\ncalibrate calib == 1: sampled; otherwise fixed at mean\nresample the resample column, kept for completeness\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.PriorSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.PriorTable","page":"API","text":"PriorTable\n\nAll rows of a prior-summary CSV, with lookup by name (spec).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.PriorTable"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ProxyData","page":"API","text":"ProxyData\n\nCO2 proxy observations (Foster et al. 2017 compilation). Ages in Myr ago, CO2 in ppmv. co2_low and co2_high are the ±1σ bounds given in the file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ProxyData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ReservoirState","page":"API","text":"ReservoirState{T}\n\nMasses (10^18 mol), isotopic compositions (per mil) and Sr ratios that are carried from one time step to the next (f90:279-294 and 529-545). Immutable: every step builds a new state, which keeps the time loop free of hidden mutation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ReservoirState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.ResolvedConstants","page":"API","text":"ResolvedConstants\n\nNumerical constants actually used by the model (ln 2, solver tolerance and damping, Sr offset, RCO2 bounds, present-day checks), built from a ModelOptions by resolve_constants. When fortran_single_literals = true each value is rounded through Float32, as gfortran stores default-REAL literals in single precision (finding F-05). Internal; not part of the public API.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.ResolvedConstants"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.StepStatus","page":"API","text":"StepStatus\n\nOutcome of one model time step.\n\nConverged: the CO2 solve converged and all checks passed.\nNotConverged: the CO2 solve reached iteration_threshold (Fortran failed_run).\nNaNEncountered: a NaN appeared inside the CO2 solve (Fortran ISNAN check).\nRCO2OutOfRange: RCO2 left rco2_bounds (f90:524).\nPresentDayCheckFailed: the present-day O2/CO2 check at t = 0 failed (f90:561).\nInvalidState: RCO2 is NaN although the Fortran raised no failure flag. Outputs are then NaN rather than Inf, exactly as in the Fortran.\n\nFor the first four failure codes, co2 and o2 are +Inf at that step and temp is left as computed (the Fortran convention, finding F-08).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.StepStatus"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TemperatureData","page":"API","text":"TemperatureData\n\nGlobal temperature reconstruction of Mills et al. (2019, Gondwana Research): time in Ma (≤ 0, i.e. negative ages), CO2 bounds (ppmv) and temperature maximum, minimum and average (°C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TemperatureData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TimeArrayPrior","page":"API","text":"TimeArrayPrior{T}\n\nCentres and standard deviations of the 12 time-varying forcings, both as TimeForcings on the same age grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TimeArrayPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TimeForcings","page":"API","text":"TimeForcings{T<:Real}\n\nThe 12 time-varying forcings of GEOCARBSULFvolc on a common age grid (Myr ago, strictly decreasing, within [0, 570]). Fields: age and one vector per name in TIME_FORCING_NAMES.\n\nConstruct by name:\n\nf = TimeForcings(age, Dict(:Sr => sr, :d13C => d13c, ...))   # all 12 names\nf = TimeForcings(age; Sr = sr, d13C = d13c, ...)\nf[:GEOG]                                                     # access by Symbol\n\nExamples\n\njulia> f = read_time_arrays(datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_arrays_NewDegassing.csv\")).center;\n\njulia> length(f.age), f.age[1], f.age[end]\n(58, 570.0, 0.0)\n\njulia> f[:GEOG] === f.GEOG\ntrue\n\nThe vectors are mutable, so the samplers can overwrite them in place (sample_time_forcings!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TimeForcings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TimeForcings-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"TimeForcings(age, d::AbstractDict; check_finite = true)\nTimeForcings(age; check_finite = true, kwargs...)\n\nBuild from a mapping name → vector. All 12 names in TIME_FORCING_NAMES must be present and no others. With check_finite = true (the default) every value must be finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TimeForcings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TimeSeriesSampler","page":"API","text":"TimeSeriesSampler\n\nSamplers of the 12 forcing time series around their centres. Both implementations apply the normalisation of lhs_sampling.R:106-112: fR, fL, fA, fAw_fA, fC are divided by their present-day value and GEOG has its present-day value subtracted.\n\nInverseWishartSampler: the R algorithm. Draw Σ ~ IW(ν, (ν−p−1)·diag(σ²)) (mean diag(σ²)), then x ~ N(μ, Σ). Cost O(p³) per forcing.\nMultivariateTSampler: the exact marginal of the above, a multivariate t, drawn in O(p) (improvements doc §3.2). Identical in distribution; it cannot return the covariance draws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TimeSeriesSampler"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TruncatedPrior","page":"API","text":"TruncatedPrior\n\nPrior of one constant parameter: an untruncated base distribution (Normal, LogNormal or Uniform) restricted to [lower, upper]. cdf_lower and cdf_upper are the base CDF values at the bounds.\n\nSamples are drawn exactly as in lhs_sampling.R:39-65: a uniform u is rescaled into [cdf_lower, cdf_upper] and mapped through the base quantile function (see quantile_from_unit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TruncatedPrior"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.TruncatedPrior-Tuple{PriorSpec}","page":"API","text":"TruncatedPrior(s::PriorSpec)\n\nPrior of a constant parameter from its CSV row. Gaussian: Normal(mean, two_sigma/2). Log-normal: LogNormal(log(mean), log(two_sigma/2)). Uniform: Uniform(lower, upper).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.TruncatedPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.Windows","page":"API","text":"Windows\n\nLower and upper bounds of a precalibration window at every model time step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.Windows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.copyto!-Tuple{TimeForcings, TimeForcings}","page":"API","text":"copyto!(dest::TimeForcings, src::TimeForcings)\n\nCopy all forcing values of src into dest (same age grid required).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.copyto!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration._constants_from_vector-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, AbstractVector}} where T<:Real","page":"API","text":"_constants_from_vector(T, v)\n\nFast constructor from a vector already in canonical order (used in the sampling loop).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration._constants_from_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.accepts-Tuple{OutboundRule, GeocarbResult, EvaluationContext}","page":"API","text":"accepts(rule, r::GeocarbResult, ctx::EvaluationContext) -> Bool\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.accepts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.acf_quantiles-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"acf_quantiles(res; lag_max = 10, probs = DEFAULT_PROBS) -> Array{Float64,3}\n\nQuantiles across accepted samples of the autocorrelation function of each forcing series: (lag_max + 1) × length(probs) × 12 (analysis_and_plots.R:660-707).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.acf_quantiles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.build_priors-Tuple{PriorTable}","page":"API","text":"build_priors(t::PriorTable) -> Vector{TruncatedPrior}\n\nPriors of the calibrated constant parameters, in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.build_priors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.build_rule-Tuple{ExperimentConfig}","page":"API","text":"build_rule(cfg::ExperimentConfig) -> AcceptanceRule\n\nAcceptance rule described by an experiment configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.build_rule"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.burial_fluxes-Tuple{Int64, GeocarbPrecalibration.ReservoirState, Any, Any, Any, TimeForcings}","page":"API","text":"burial_fluxes(i, s, src, capd34S, capd13C, f) -> NamedTuple\n\nBurial fluxes of pyrite (Fbp), organic carbon (Fbg), CaSO4 sulfur (Fbs) and carbonate carbon (Fbc) from isotopic mass balance (f90:383-390).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.burial_fluxes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.bw_nrd0-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API","text":"bw_nrd0(x) -> Float64\n\nSilverman's rule-of-thumb bandwidth, identical to R's bw.nrd0: 0.9·min(sd, IQR/1.34)·n^(−1/5), with R's fallbacks for degenerate data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.bw_nrd0"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.calibrated_specs-Tuple{PriorTable}","page":"API","text":"Constant-parameter rows that are calibrated (calib == 1), in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.calibrated_specs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.carbonate_fBB-Tuple{Real, Any, Any, Any, ConstantParams}","page":"API","text":"carbonate_fBB(t, rco2_prev, gcm, GEOG, p) -> fBB\n\nEffect of climate and CO2 on carbonate weathering (Berner 2004 eqs. 2.6-2.7, 2.29), using RCO2 from the previous time step (f90:325, 336-343). Between 380 and 350 Ma the non-vascular and vascular expressions are blended linearly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.carbonate_fBB"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.climate_sensitivity_summary-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"climate_sensitivity_summary(res; probs = (0.05, 0.5, 0.95), exceed = (6.0, 7.0)) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe headline numbers of Wong et al. (2021): quantiles of ΔT2x, GLAC and the glacial sensitivity ΔT2x·GLAC, and Pr(ΔT2x ≥ x) for each x in exceed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.climate_sensitivity_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.co2_windows-Tuple{ProxyData, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"co2_windows(proxy::ProxyData, age; nsig = 1.0, bounds = (0.0, 50_000.0)) -> Windows\n\nCO2 windows (constraints.R:28-43). For each model step, the proxies within ±dt/2 of its age are pooled. Each proxy spans log10(co2) ± nsig·log10(co2_high/co2), and the window is the envelope of those spans, intersected with bounds. Steps without data get the full bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.co2_windows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.column-Tuple{GeocarbPrecalibration.CSVTable, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"column(t::CSVTable, name) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe raw (stripped) cells of a column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.column"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.config_summary-Tuple{ExperimentConfig}","page":"API","text":"config_summary(cfg::ExperimentConfig) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nTOML-friendly summary of a configuration (stored as provenance with every result).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.config_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.constant_column-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"constant_column(res, name::Symbol) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nAccepted values of one constant parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.constant_column"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.constant_specs-Tuple{PriorTable}","page":"API","text":"Constant-parameter rows, in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.constant_specs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.correlation_screen-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API","text":"correlation_screen(res, target::AbstractVector; method = :spearman) -> NamedTuple\n\nCorrelation of target (one value per accepted sample, for example the temperature at 100 Ma or ΔT2x) with every constant parameter, sorted by decreasing magnitude (analysis_and_plots.R:553-656). Returns (names, r).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.correlation_screen"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.datapath-Tuple{Vararg{AbstractString}}","page":"API","text":"datapath(parts...) -> String\n\nPath of a file in the package's data/ directory, for example datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_all.csv\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.datapath"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.default_tag-Tuple{Symbol, Real}","page":"API","text":"default_tag(constraint, threshold; suffix = \"\", seed = nothing) -> String\n\nResult tag in the style of the R code: \"<c|t|ct>_out<percent>\", for example \"ct_out30\", followed by _<suffix> and _seed<seed> when given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.default_tag"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.forcing_matrix-Union{Tuple{TimeForcings{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"API","text":"forcing_matrix(f::TimeForcings) -> Matrix\n\nn_time × 12 matrix with the forcings as columns in TIME_FORCING_NAMES order (the layout of the Fortran Matrix_12).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.forcing_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.forcing_series-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"forcing_series(res, name::Symbol) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nn_time × n accepted series of one forcing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.forcing_series"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.gaussian_kde-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API","text":"gaussian_kde(x; from = minimum(x) - 3bw, to = maximum(x) + 3bw, n = 512, bw = bw_nrd0(x))\n    -> (x = grid, density = values)\n\nGaussian kernel density estimate on an evenly spaced grid of n points, evaluated exactly (R's density uses an FFT approximation of the same estimator).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.gaussian_kde"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.gcm_factor-Tuple{Real, ConstantParams, ModelOptions, GeocarbPrecalibration.ResolvedConstants}","page":"API","text":"gcm_factor(t, p, o, k) -> GCM\n\nClimate-sensitivity factor GCM (\"capital gamma\" in Berner 2004), with deltaT2X = GCM·ln 2:\n\nin glacial intervals: GCM = GLAC·deltaT2X / ln 2;\notherwise, if o.dT2X_ramp and t ∈ (end, start] of o.dT2X_ramp_interval, the supplemental ramp: fac = (GLAC·(start − t) + (t − end)) / (start − end), GCM = fac·deltaT2X / ln 2 (run_geocarb_supp.f90:324-336);\notherwise GCM = deltaT2X / ln 2.\n\nk are the ResolvedConstants (they carry ln 2, possibly rounded to single precision as in the Fortran).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.gcm_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.has_column-Tuple{GeocarbPrecalibration.CSVTable, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"has_column(t::CSVTable, name) -> Bool\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.has_column"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.hindcast-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"hindcast(res::PrecalibrationResult; options = nothing, T0 = 15.0, include_geog = true,\n         n = nothing, rng = StableRNG(1)) -> (co2, temp, idx)\n\nModel CO2 (ppmv) and temperature (°C) of the accepted samples, as n_time × n matrices. Temperatures are temp + T0 plus, with include_geog = true, the sample's GEOG series, which is what the R analysis plots against proxies (analysis_and_plots.R:140).\n\nWith options = nothing, the model outputs saved during precalibration are reused (no model runs). Pass a ModelOptions to re-run every sample with different model options, for example the supplemental-experiment variants. n subsamples as in parameter_quantiles; idx are the samples used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.hindcast"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.in_glacial_interval-Tuple{Real, ModelOptions}","page":"API","text":"in_glacial_interval(t, o::ModelOptions) -> Bool\n\ntrue if age t (Myr ago) lies in one of the closed intervals o.glacial_intervals. With the defaults this is (260 ≤ t ≤ 330) || t ≤ 40 (f90:309). That follows Fielding et al. (2008); the original BASIC code used 270–340 Ma.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.in_glacial_interval"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.initial_state-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, ConstantParams}} where T<:Real","page":"API","text":"initial_state(T, p::ConstantParams) -> ReservoirState{T}\n\nState at 570 Ma. dlsa and dlca follow from isotopic mass balance (f90:291-292).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.initial_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.inverse_wishart_dof-Tuple{TimeForcings, AbstractDict{Symbol, <:Real}}","page":"API","text":"inverse_wishart_dof(sigma::TimeForcings, f_var_red) -> Dict{Symbol,Int}\n\nDegrees of freedom of the inverse-Wishart prior of each forcing's covariance (time_series_df.R:19-21):\n\nν = n_time + 3 + ceil(2·max(σ²) / (1 − f))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.inverse_wishart_dof"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.isfailure-Tuple{StepStatus}","page":"API","text":"isfailure(s::StepStatus) -> Bool\n\ntrue for the statuses that make the Fortran write +Inf into co2 and o2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.isfailure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.isotope_fractionations-Tuple{Any, ConstantParams}","page":"API","text":"isotope_fractionations(oxygen, p) -> (CAPd34S, CAPd13C)\n\nIsotopic fractionations between sulfate and pyrite sulfur, and between carbonate and organic carbon, as functions of the O2 mass (Berner 2006a; f90:381-382).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.isotope_fractionations"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.legacy_percent_outbound-Tuple{AbstractVector, Windows}","page":"API","text":"legacy_percent_outbound(series, w::Windows) -> Float64\n\nFraction of time steps at which series lies outside the window, with the exact semantics of percent_outbound.R (Mills et al. 2019):\n\nif any value is ±Inf (a failed Fortran step), return 1.0;\notherwise, if any value is NaN, return NaN (R returns NA; a run with NaN never satisfies ≤ threshold);\notherwise count(x < low | x > high) / n.\n\nBecause the Fortran never sets temperatures to Inf, a temperature series of a failed run can still score below the threshold (finding F-04). Use percent_outbound with reject_failed = true to avoid that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.legacy_percent_outbound"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.linear_interpolator-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API","text":"linear_interpolator(x, y) -> function\n\nPiecewise-linear interpolation through (x, y), returning NaN outside [minimum(x), maximum(x)] (R's approxfun with rule = 1). Used for the Park & Royer (2011) ΔT2x distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.linear_interpolator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.load_experiments-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_experiments(path) -> Vector{ExperimentConfig}\n\nRead an experiment TOML file. Without a [grid] table it describes one experiment. With a [grid] table, one experiment is created for every combination of grid.constraint (outer loop) and grid.threshold (inner loop), in that order, as expand.grid did in lhs_driver.R. Seeds are then\n\nseed_multiplier * index with index = 1, 2, … (the R rule 1234*idx_experiment), or\none experiment per entry of grid.seeds, or\nthe top-level seed.\n\nRelative data-file names are resolved against data_dir (default: the package's data/ directory). output_dir and time_center_from are relative to the current working directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.load_experiments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.load_result-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_result(path) -> PrecalibrationResult\n\nRead a file written by save_result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.load_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.mean_constants-Tuple{PriorTable}","page":"API","text":"mean_constants(t::PriorTable) -> ConstantParams{Float64}\n\nAll constants at their prior means (the \"preliminary simulation\" of lhs.R:30).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.mean_constants"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.mean_time_forcings-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Real, 3}, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"mean_time_forcings(series::AbstractArray{<:Real,3}, age) -> TimeForcings\n\nMean forcings from an n_time × n_samples × 12 array (forcings in TIME_FORCING_NAMES order), for example the accepted series of a previous experiment. This is what the two-stage Cretaceous experiment uses as new centres (the R version, par_time_mean_cret.csv, is affected by finding F-27).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.mean_time_forcings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.merge_results-Tuple{AbstractVector{PrecalibrationResult}}","page":"API","text":"merge_results(results; tag = results[1].tag) -> PrecalibrationResult\n\nConcatenate the accepted samples of several results on the same age grid, for example the three seeds of the 30 % CO2 + temperature experiment (analysis_and_plots.R:61-77). n_simulated is summed; covariances are kept only if every result has them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.merge_results"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.model_options_from_dict-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"model_options_from_dict(d::AbstractDict) -> ModelOptions\n\nBuild options from a dictionary with string or symbol keys (for example the [model] table of an experiment TOML file). Unknown keys raise an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.model_options_from_dict"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.n_accepted-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"n_accepted(res::PrecalibrationResult) -> Int\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.n_accepted"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.nanlog-Tuple{Real}","page":"API","text":"nanlog(x)\n\nlog(x), except that a negative x returns NaN instead of throwing a DomainError. nanlog(0.0) == -Inf, as in Fortran.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.nanlog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.nanpow-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"API","text":"nanpow(x, y)\n\nx^y, except that a negative x with a non-integer y returns NaN (as C pow and Fortran ** do) instead of throwing a DomainError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.nanpow"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.numeric_column-Tuple{GeocarbPrecalibration.CSVTable, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"numeric_column(t::CSVTable, name; allow_missing = false) -> Vector{Float64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.numeric_column"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.numeric_column-Tuple{GeocarbPrecalibration.CSVTable, Integer}","page":"API","text":"numeric_column(t::CSVTable, j::Integer; allow_missing = false) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nNumeric column by position (for files whose header names are not meaningful).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.numeric_column"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.options_dict-Tuple{ModelOptions}","page":"API","text":"options_dict(o::ModelOptions) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nTOML-friendly representation of the options (inverse of model_options_from_dict).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.options_dict"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.parameter_description-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"API","text":"parameter_description(name::Symbol) -> String\n\nPhysical meaning of a constant parameter or time-varying forcing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.parameter_description"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.parameter_quantiles-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"parameter_quantiles(res; probs = DEFAULT_PROBS, n = nothing, rng = StableRNG(1))\n    -> (names, probs, values)\n\nQuantiles of every accepted constant parameter, plus the derived glacial sensitivity :deltaT2Xglac = deltaT2X·GLAC as the last row. values[i, j] is the probs[j] quantile of names[i]. With n, a subsample of n accepted samples is used, as the R analysis did to compare experiments of different sizes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.parameter_quantiles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.parameter_units-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"API","text":"parameter_units(name::Symbol) -> String\n\nUnits of a constant parameter or time-varying forcing (\"-\" for dimensionless).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.parameter_units"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.parameter_vector-Union{Tuple{ConstantParams{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"API","text":"parameter_vector(p::ConstantParams) -> Vector\n\nThe parameter values in the canonical order of CONSTANT_PARAMETER_NAMES.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.parameter_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.parse_number-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"parse_number(s; allow_missing = false) -> Float64\n\nParse a numeric cell. Accepts inf, Inf, -inf and the legacy _inf (minus infinity) used in the prior CSV files. Empty cells and NA become NaN when allow_missing = true and raise an error otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.parse_number"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.percent_outbound-Tuple{GeocarbResult, Windows, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"percent_outbound(r::GeocarbResult, w::Windows, variable; T0 = 15.0, reject_failed = false) -> Float64\n\n% outbound of a model run for variable = :co2 (compared as is) or :temp (compared as r.temp .+ T0, see temperature). With reject_failed = true, any run with a failed step scores 1.0, whatever the variable. With false (the default), the result equals legacy_percent_outbound.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.percent_outbound"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_co2_windows_and_proxies","page":"API","text":"plot_co2_windows_and_proxies(proxy::ProxyData, co2::Windows) -> Figure\n\nCO2 windows and proxy data points (R figure co2_windows_and_proxies.pdf). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_co2_windows_and_proxies"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_deltaT2X_distributions","page":"API","text":"plot_deltaT2X_distributions(res; prior = nothing, park_royer = nothing) -> Figure\n\nPosterior density of ΔT2x (and ΔT2x·GLAC), with the prior and the Park & Royer (2011) distribution (R figure deltaT2X_distributions.pdf). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_deltaT2X_distributions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_deltaT2X_experiments","page":"API","text":"plot_deltaT2X_experiments(results::AbstractDict{String,PrecalibrationResult}) -> Figure\n\nΔT2x quantile ranges across experiments (R figure deltaT2X_experiments.pdf). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_deltaT2X_experiments"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_gym_modification","page":"API","text":"plot_gym_modification(; GYM = 0.875, LIFE = 0.25, options = ModelOptions(gym_angio_transition = (110, 60), gym_factor = 0.25)) -> Figure\n\nfE through time for the default and a modified vegetation history (R figure gym_modification.pdf). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_gym_modification"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_model_vs_proxy","page":"API","text":"plot_model_vs_proxy(res, proxy::ProxyData, temp_windows::Windows; probs_bands = ...) -> Figure\n\nHindcast CO2 and temperature bands against CO2 proxies and temperature windows (R figure model_vs_proxy.pdf). Requires a Makie backend (using CairoMakie).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_model_vs_proxy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_time_series_autocorrelations","page":"API","text":"plot_time_series_autocorrelations(res; lag_max = 10) -> Figure\n\nAutocorrelation quantiles of the accepted forcing series (R figure time_series_autocorrelations.pdf). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_time_series_autocorrelations"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_time_series_parameters","page":"API","text":"plot_time_series_parameters(res, prior_quantiles) -> Figure\n\nPrior and precalibrated 95 % bands of the 12 forcings (R figure time_series_parameters.pdf). Requires a Makie backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.plot_time_series_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.prepare_precalibration-Tuple{ExperimentConfig}","page":"API","text":"prepare_precalibration(cfg::ExperimentConfig) -> PrecalibrationSetup\n\nRead all inputs and build the priors, samplers, windows and acceptance rule of an experiment. Called by run_precalibration; exposed for inspection and tests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.prepare_precalibration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.prior_pdf-Tuple{TruncatedPrior, Real}","page":"API","text":"prior_pdf(p::TruncatedPrior, x) -> Float64\n\nDensity of the truncated prior at x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.prior_pdf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.prior_posterior_table-Tuple{PriorTable, PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"prior_posterior_table(table::PriorTable, res; probs = (0.05, 0.95)) -> NamedTuple\n\nPrior mean and 5–95 % range against posterior median and 5–95 % range for every calibrated constant (som_parameters_table.csv, analysis_and_plots.R:485-537). The prior quantiles are those of the truncated priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.prior_posterior_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.proxy_step_index-Tuple{ProxyData, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"proxy_step_index(d::ProxyData, age::AbstractVector) -> Vector{Union{Int,Nothing}}\n\nModel time step that each observation is compared against: the step whose age a satisfies a - dt/2 ≤ obs_age < a + dt/2 (getData.R:28-35), or nothing if none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.proxy_step_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.quantile_from_cdf-Tuple{TruncatedPrior, Real}","page":"API","text":"quantile_from_cdf(p::TruncatedPrior, q) -> x\n\nBase quantile at an already-rescaled probability q ∈ [F(lower), F(upper)] (the parameters_lhs values of the R code).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.quantile_from_cdf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.quantile_from_unit-Tuple{TruncatedPrior, Real}","page":"API","text":"quantile_from_unit(p::TruncatedPrior, u) -> x\n\nMap u ∈ (0, 1) to a parameter value: quantile(base, u·(F(upper) − F(lower)) + F(lower)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.quantile_from_unit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.random_lhs-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Integer, Integer}","page":"API","text":"random_lhs(rng, n, d) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nRandom Latin hypercube design of n points in (0, 1)^d, the algorithm of R's lhs::randomLHS. For each column, a random permutation of the strata 1..n is jittered uniformly within each stratum: (perm[i] − u)/n.\n\nEvery value lies strictly inside its stratum ((k−1)/n, k/n), so an unbounded prior never receives exactly 0 or 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.random_lhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.read_csv_table-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_csv_table(path) -> CSVTable\n\nRead a comma-separated file with a header line. Blank lines are skipped. Rows with a different number of fields than the header raise an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.read_csv_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.read_park_royer_2011-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_park_royer_2011(path) -> NamedTuple\n\nRead ParkRoyer2011_Fig3_85varred.csv (Park & Royer 2011, Fig. 3: distribution of ΔT2x). Returns (deltaT2X, pdf, cdf). The file has CR-only line endings and a byte-order mark, both handled by read_csv_table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.read_park_royer_2011"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.read_prior_table-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_prior_table(path) -> PriorTable\n\nRead and validate a prior-summary CSV (GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_all.csv). The file must contain exactly the 56 constants of CONSTANT_PARAMETER_NAMES and the 12 time arrays of TIME_FORCING_NAMES, in any order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.read_prior_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.read_proxy_data-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_proxy_data(path; proxies = (:paleosols, :alkenones, :stomata, :boron, :liverworts)) -> ProxyData\n\nRead a CO2 proxy CSV (columns age, co2, co2_low, co2_high, proxy_type, reference; other columns are ignored) and keep the listed proxy types. As in getData.R:12-20, rows are grouped by proxy type in the order of proxies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.read_proxy_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.read_temperature_data-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_temperature_data(path) -> TemperatureData\n\nRead Mills_GR_2019_temp_co2.csv. As in constraints.R:52, the six columns are taken by position: time, CO2 max, CO2 min, T max, T min, T average. Empty cells become NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.read_temperature_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.read_time_arrays-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_time_arrays(path; use_godderis = true) -> TimeArrayPrior{Float64}\n\nRead a GEOCARB_input_arrays_*.csv file: an age column and, for each forcing X, a centre column X and a standard-deviation column eX. This reproduces parameterSetup.R:34-85:\n\nwith use_godderis = true (the R default l_Godderis <- TRUE), fA, fAw_fA, fD, GEOG and their errors are taken from the *_Godderis columns (Goddéris et al. 2012);\nthe centre of fAw_fA is divided by its present-day value (Krause et al. 2018); its standard deviation is not rescaled, as in the R code.\n\nThe file must list ages from oldest to youngest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.read_time_arrays"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.read_variance_reduction","page":"API","text":"read_variance_reduction(path = datapath(\"variance_reduction.toml\")) -> Dict{Symbol,Float64}\n\nVariance-reduction factors f per forcing (time_series_df.R:12). They set the inverse-Wishart degrees of freedom so that sampled variances match the reduced-variance experiments of Royer et al. (2014).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.read_variance_reduction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.row_quantiles-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Real}}","page":"API","text":"row_quantiles(M; probs = DEFAULT_PROBS) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nQuantiles of every row of M, ignoring NaN (rows that are all NaN give NaN). ±Inf values are kept, as in R's quantile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.row_quantiles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.run_geocarb!-Tuple{GeocarbResult, ConstantParams, TimeForcings}","page":"API","text":"run_geocarb!(out::GeocarbResult, p, f; options = ModelOptions()) -> out\n\nIn-place version of run_geocarb: overwrite the pre-allocated out (see GeocarbResult{T}(age)). It does not allocate, which matters inside the sampling loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.run_geocarb!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.run_geocarb-Tuple{ConstantParams, TimeForcings}","page":"API","text":"run_geocarb(p::ConstantParams, f::TimeForcings; options = ModelOptions()) -> GeocarbResult\n\nRun GEOCARBSULFvolc from 570 Ma to the present on the age grid of f.\n\nThis is a line-by-line port of fortran/src/run_geocarb.f90 (and, through ModelOptions, of run_geocarb_supp.f90). The model never throws because of the parameter values: numerical failures are reported per step in result.status (see StepStatus) with the Fortran output conventions (co2 = o2 = Inf on failed steps). Invalid inputs (for example an age grid whose spacing is not options.dt) raise an ArgumentError before the run starts.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> table = read_prior_table(datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_all.csv\"));\n\njulia> p = mean_constants(table);\n\njulia> f = read_time_arrays(datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_arrays_NewDegassing.csv\")).center;\n\njulia> r = run_geocarb(p, f);\n\njulia> succeeded(r)\ntrue\n\njulia> length(r.co2)   # ppmv, 58 values from 570 Ma to the present\n58\n\njulia> round.(r.co2[1:3]; sigdigits = 6)\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 5473.6\n 5663.08\n 6226.25\n\njulia> round(r.co2[end]; digits = 1)   # present day\n243.7\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.run_geocarb"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.run_precalibration-Tuple{ExperimentConfig}","page":"API","text":"run_precalibration(cfg::ExperimentConfig; max_parallel = Threads.nthreads(),\n                   checkpoint_dir = nothing, verbose = true) -> PrecalibrationResult\n\nRun one precalibration experiment:\n\nread the priors, forcing arrays, proxy data and temperature data;\nin chunks of cfg.n_per_chunk, sample constants (Latin hypercube through the truncated priors) and forcing series, run the model, and keep the samples that pass the acceptance rule;\nstop once cfg.n_accept_min samples are accepted (keeping exactly that many) or after cfg.n_sample simulations.\n\nChunks are independent: chunk c uses its own StableRNG seeded from (cfg.seed, c). Up to max_parallel chunks run at the same time on separate threads, and their results are consumed in chunk order, so the result does not depend on the number of threads. With checkpoint_dir, every finished chunk is written there and reused if the run is restarted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.run_precalibration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.sample_constants-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, AbstractVector{<:TruncatedPrior}, Integer}","page":"API","text":"sample_constants(rng, priors, n) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nn × length(priors) matrix of parameter values from one Latin hypercube design transformed through the truncated priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.sample_constants"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.sample_constants_params-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult, Integer}","page":"API","text":"sample_constants_params(res, k) -> ConstantParams{Float64}\n\nConstant parameters of accepted sample k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.sample_constants_params"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.sample_forcings-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult, Integer}","page":"API","text":"sample_forcings(res, k) -> TimeForcings{Float64}\n\nForcings of accepted sample k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.sample_forcings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.sample_time_forcings!-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, TimeForcings, TimeSeriesSampler}","page":"API","text":"sample_time_forcings!(rng, out::TimeForcings, sampler; covariances = nothing) -> out\n\nOverwrite out with one draw of all 12 forcings. With an InverseWishartSampler, covariances may be a Dict{Symbol,Matrix{Float64}} that receives the covariance draw of each forcing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.sample_time_forcings!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.save_result-Tuple{AbstractString, PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"save_result(path, res::PrecalibrationResult) -> path\n\nWrite a result to a JLD2 file (HDF5-based; also readable from Python/R HDF5 tools). Only plain arrays, strings and dictionaries of strings are stored, so the file does not depend on Julia type definitions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.save_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.shift-Tuple{Windows, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"shift(w::Windows, delta::AbstractVector) -> Windows\n\nWindow moved by delta at every step (for example to add the solar term back in for plotting; constraints.R:72-73 did this with the fixed prior mean of Ws, finding F-19).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.shift"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.silicate_weathering_no_climate-Tuple{Int64, Any, Any, Any, Any, ConstantParams, TimeForcings}","page":"API","text":"silicate_weathering_no_climate(i, src, bur, fE, fvolc, p, f) -> fwsi_no_climate\n\nSilicate weathering at time t relative to the present without climatic effects: the silicate weathering required by mass balance (burial − carbonate weathering) divided by the non-climatic factors times Fwsi_0 (Berner 2004 eq. 5.2; \"fB\" in the BASIC code; f90:416).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.silicate_weathering_no_climate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.solve_rco2-Tuple{Real, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, ConstantParams, ModelOptions, GeocarbPrecalibration.ResolvedConstants}","page":"API","text":"solve_rco2(t, rco2, fwsi_nc, gcm, RT, GEOG, p, o, k) -> (rco2, status, iterations)\n\nDamped Newton solve for RCO2 (CO2 relative to 250 ppmv) at age t, starting from the previous step's value. Returns the new RCO2, a StepStatus (Converged, NotConverged or NaNEncountered) and the number of iterations.\n\nThe convergence test, damping, fallback shrinkage and failure rules are those of the Fortran (f90:431-521). In particular, convergence is judged on the relative step size (abs(RCO2/RCO2_old − 1) ≤ tol), and the iteration at which count == iteration_threshold is counted as a failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.solve_rco2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.source_fluxes-Tuple{Int64, GeocarbPrecalibration.ReservoirState, Any, Any, ConstantParams, TimeForcings}","page":"API","text":"source_fluxes(i, s, fE, fBB, p, f) -> NamedTuple\n\nWeathering and degassing fluxes at time step i (10^18 mol/Myr; f90:363-378; see Berner 2006a pp. 5660-5661 for the Fwp, Fws and Fwg fluxes). Returned fields: Fwpy, Fwsy, Fwgy, Fwcy (young pyrite, CaSO4, organic C, carbonate C), Fwpa, Fwsa, Fwga, Fwca (old reservoirs), Fmp, Fms, Fmg, Fmc (degassing) and Fyop, Fyos, Fyog, Fyoc (degassing + old-reservoir weathering).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.source_fluxes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.spec-Tuple{PriorTable, Symbol}","page":"API","text":"spec(t::PriorTable, name::Symbol) -> PriorSpec\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.spec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.subsample_indices-Tuple{Integer, Any}","page":"API","text":"subsample_indices(n_available, n; rng = StableRNG(1)) -> Vector{Int}\n\nn sample indices: without replacement if enough samples exist, otherwise with replacement (analysis_and_plots.R:84-88). n = nothing keeps all samples in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.subsample_indices"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.succeeded-Tuple{GeocarbResult}","page":"API","text":"succeeded(r::GeocarbResult) -> Bool\n\ntrue if every time step converged and passed all checks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.succeeded"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.survival_curve-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API","text":"survival_curve(x) -> (x = sorted values, survival = 1 − ecdf)\n\nEmpirical survival function with ecdf = (1:n)/(n + 1) (analysis_supplemental_experiments.R:244-252).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.survival_curve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.temperature-Tuple{GeocarbResult}","page":"API","text":"temperature(r::GeocarbResult; T0 = 15.0, geog = nothing, Ws = nothing) -> Vector\n\nGlobal mean surface temperature implied by a model run, with the conventions of the original code made explicit (finding F-20):\n\nr.temp is GCM·ln(RCO2) − Ws·t/570: warming from CO2, minus the solar term.\ntemperature(r) adds the present-day temperature T0 (15 °C, Berner 2004). This is the quantity compared with the Mills et al. (2019) windows during precalibration (lhs_sampling.R:137).\ntemperature(r; geog = f.GEOG) also adds the palaeogeography term. This is what the R analysis scripts plot as hindcasts (analysis_and_plots.R:140).\ntemperature(r; Ws = p.Ws) adds the solar-luminosity term back in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.temperature_windows-Tuple{TemperatureData, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"temperature_windows(data::TemperatureData, age; nsig = 1.0) -> Windows\n\nTemperature windows from Mills et al. (2019) (constraints.R:51-71): [T_avg − nsig·(T_avg − T_min), T_avg + nsig·(T_max − T_avg)] at the record whose time equals −age. The windows include the solar-luminosity effect and are compared with temp + 15 from the model (see temperature).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.temperature_windows"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.time_series_quantiles-Tuple{PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"time_series_quantiles(res; probs = DEFAULT_PROBS) -> Array{Float64,3}\n\nn_time × length(probs) × 12 quantiles of the accepted forcing series.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.time_series_quantiles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.transform_unit-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Real}, AbstractVector{<:TruncatedPrior}}","page":"API","text":"transform_unit(U, priors) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nMap unit-hypercube points to parameter values with quantile_from_unit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.transform_unit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.truncated_distribution-Tuple{TruncatedPrior}","page":"API","text":"truncated_distribution(p::TruncatedPrior)\n\nThe equivalent Distributions.truncated object (useful for densities and tests).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.truncated_distribution"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.update-Tuple{ConstantParams}","page":"API","text":"update(p::ConstantParams; kwargs...) -> ConstantParams\n\nCopy of p with some parameters replaced, e.g. update(p; deltaT2X = 4.0). The element type is promoted if needed, so automatic-differentiation numbers can be passed in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.update"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.update_oxygen-Tuple{Real, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"API","text":"update_oxygen(t, oxygen, src, bur, dt) -> oxygen\n\nAtmospheric O2 mass for this step (f90:403-405). The first step (t = 570) keeps the initial value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.update_oxygen"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.update_reservoirs-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{GeocarbPrecalibration.ReservoirState{T}, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, ConstantParams, Any}} where T","page":"API","text":"update_reservoirs(s, oxygen, src, bur, capd34S, capd13C, roc, d34S, d13C, p, dt)\n\nNew masses and isotopic values for the next time step (f90:529-545). The updates are sequential, as in the Fortran: each line uses the values updated on the lines before it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.update_reservoirs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.validate-Tuple{ExperimentConfig}","page":"API","text":"validate(cfg::ExperimentConfig) -> cfg\n\nThrow an ArgumentError describing the first problem found (missing files, an inconsistent sample size, unknown choices, ...).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.validate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.validate-Tuple{ModelOptions}","page":"API","text":"validate(o::ModelOptions) -> o\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if the options are inconsistent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.validate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.vegetation_fE-Tuple{Real, ConstantParams, ModelOptions}","page":"API","text":"vegetation_fE(t, p, o) -> fE\n\nEffect of plants on the weathering rate at age t relative to the present (f90:319-359; supplemental variant run_geocarb_supp.f90:374-395):\n\nage (Myr ago) fE\n(380, 570] LIFE (non-vascular plants)\n(350, 380] linear ramp LIFE → GYM\n(start, 350] GYM (gymnosperms)\n(end, start] linear ramp gym_factor·GYM → 1\n[0, end] 1 (angiosperms)\n\n(start, end) = o.gym_angio_transition, default (130, 80). The Fortran checks the intervals one after another, with later checks overriding earlier ones; that order is kept. Unlike the Fortran supplemental code, GYM is never modified in place, so gym_factor = 0 is well defined (finding F-09).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.vegetation_fE"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.volcanic_weathering-Tuple{Int64, Any, Any, GeocarbPrecalibration.ReservoirState, ConstantParams, TimeForcings, GeocarbPrecalibration.ResolvedConstants}","page":"API","text":"volcanic_weathering(i, src, bur, s, p, f, k) -> (Roc, Xvolc, fvolc)\n\nVolcanic / non-volcanic silicate weathering, the \"volc\" in GEOCARBSULFvolc (Berner 2006b, 2008; f90:393-400):\n\nRoc: 87Sr/86Sr of seawater as recorded in carbonates, Sr/10^4 + 0.7;\nRg: 87Sr/86Sr of non-volcanic silicates (Rnv in Berner 2006b);\nXvolc: fraction of Ca and Mg silicate weathering derived from volcanic rocks;\nfvolc: volcanic weathering effect relative to the present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.volcanic_weathering"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.volcanic_weathering_factor-Tuple{Any, ConstantParams}","page":"API","text":"volcanic_weathering_factor(Xvolc, p) -> fvolc\n\nfvolc = (VNV·Xvolc + 1 − Xvolc) / (VNV·Xvolc_0 + 1 − Xvolc_0), equal to 1 when Xvolc == Xvolc_0 (f90:400).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.volcanic_weathering_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.with_overrides-Tuple{ExperimentConfig}","page":"API","text":"with_overrides(cfg::ExperimentConfig; kwargs...) -> ExperimentConfig\n\nCopy of cfg with some fields replaced, for example with_overrides(cfg; n_sample = 1000, n_per_chunk = 100).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.with_overrides"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.write_csv-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"API","text":"write_csv(path, header, columns)\n\nWrite columns (vectors of equal length) with a header line. Numbers are written with repr, so they round-trip exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.write_csv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#GeocarbPrecalibration.write_summary_csvs-Tuple{AbstractString, PrecalibrationResult}","page":"API","text":"write_summary_csvs(dir, res::PrecalibrationResult) -> Vector{String}\n\nWrite human-readable CSV summaries next to the JLD2 file: constants.csv (accepted constants, one row per sample), prcout.csv (% outbound, sample keys) and provenance.toml. Returns the paths written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.write_summary_csvs"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"How fast the Julia port is, compared with the original R/Fortran code, is measured by the timing benchmark in benchmark/timing (plan: TIMING_BENCHMARK_PLAN.md; usage: benchmark/timing/README.md). It times every stage of the workflow in both implementations on the same machine, checks the results of every timed block, and writes a report with medians, interquartile ranges and 95 % bootstrap intervals for the speed-ups.\n\nrun_timing.bat --suite quick          (Windows)\njulia --project=benchmark/timing benchmark/timing/run_timing.jl --suite quick\n\nNo numbers are given on this page yet: they depend on the machine, and the reference numbers will be taken from a standard run on the machine that ran the legacy comparison. When they are added, they will come with that run's env.toml (CPU, RAM, OS, Julia and R versions, BLAS, gfortran flags, power plan and antivirus state).","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#What-is-measured","page":"Performance","text":"family what plan IDs\nstart-up fresh process, package loading, input set-up, first model call, first figure S1-S3, G1\nmodel the model alone (Julia vs the Fortran library called directly), R's wrapper layers, supplemental variants, cost per sample against solver iterations, model options, Fortran build flags M1-M6\nsampling Latin hypercube, prior transform, time-series samplers by stage, covariance storage, raw RNG P1-P5\nconstraints % outbound, acceptance C1-C2\nengine fixed-work precalibration and where its time goes, realistic stopping, threads, peak memory, checkpoints E1-E6\nI/O saving and loading results O1-O3\nanalysis and figures the numerical sections and the figures of analysis_and_plots.R on a shared ensemble A1-A6, G2-G3\nend to end set-up, precalibration, save, analysis and figures in one fresh process X1","title":"What is measured"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Instrumentation-in-the-package","page":"Performance","text":"run_precalibration contains timing sections written with TimerOutputs.@timeit_debug. They compile to nothing unless switched on:\n\nusing GeocarbPrecalibration, TimerOutputs\nTimerOutputs.enable_debug_timings(GeocarbPrecalibration)\nrun_precalibration(cfg; max_parallel = 1)\nGeocarbPrecalibration.TIMER          # the accumulated sections\nTimerOutputs.disable_debug_timings(GeocarbPrecalibration)\n\nUse them with one thread only: the timer is shared between chunks.","title":"Instrumentation in the package"},{"category":"section","location":"temperature_conventions/#Temperature-conventions","page":"Temperature conventions","text":"The R code used three different temperatures, documented only in comments that contradict each other (finding F-20). In Julia they are made explicit by temperature:\n\nquantity Julia R\nmodel output: CO2 warming minus the solar term r.temp = GCM·ln(RCO2) − Ws·t/570 temp_out in run_geocarb.f90:556\ncompared with the Mills et al. (2019) windows temperature(r) = r.temp .+ 15 model_out[,\"temp\"] + 15 (lhs_sampling.R:137)\nplotted as hindcasts temperature(r; geog = f.GEOG) temp + GEOG + 15 (analysis_and_plots.R:140)\nwith the solar term added back temperature(r; Ws = p.Ws) commented out in the R code\n\nT0 = 15 °C is the present-day temperature assumed by Berner (2004, eqs. 2.8 and 2.28).","title":"Temperature conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"porting_notes/#Porting-notes","page":"Porting notes","text":"This page records how the R/Fortran code maps onto the Julia package and every decision that makes the two differ. Finding numbers (F-xx) refer to the review of the original code carried out before the port, and decision numbers (D-x) to the porting decisions taken from it (internal planning documents, not distributed). Each finding used in the code is described where it is cited.","title":"Porting notes"},{"category":"section","location":"porting_notes/#File-mapping","page":"Porting notes","text":"R / Fortran Julia\nfortran/src/run_geocarb.f90 src/model/*.jl (run_geocarb)\nfortran/src/run_geocarb_supp.f90 the same code with ModelOptions\nrun_geocarbF.R, run_geocarb_suppF.R, model_run*.R removed: no FFI, no index reordering\nparameterSetup.R read_prior_table, read_time_arrays, src/parameters/names.jl\nmodel_setup.R, getData.R ExperimentConfig, read_proxy_data\nconstraints.R, percent_outbound.R co2_windows, temperature_windows, legacy_percent_outbound\ntime_series_df.R inverse_wishart_dof, data/variance_reduction.toml\nlhs*.R, lhs_sampling*.R run_precalibration, AcceptanceRules\nlhs_driver*.R, run_experiment.R experiments/**/*.toml, scripts/run_experiment.jl\nanalysis_and_plots.R src/analysis/*.jl, ext/GeocarbMakieExt.jl, scripts/analysis_main.jl\nanalysis_supplemental_experiments.R scripts/analysis_supplemental.jl\ncretaceous_experiment.R scripts/analysis_cretaceous.jl, experiments/cretaceous/stage2.toml\ninstall_packages.R Project.toml\n\nThe untouched originals are kept in legacy/ as the test oracle until parity is signed off.","title":"File mapping"},{"category":"section","location":"porting_notes/#Decisions-adopted-(plan-§9,-proposed-defaults)","page":"Porting notes","text":" decision implementation\nD1 keep known quirks for parity; fixes are opt-in fortran_single_literals = true (F-05, the v1 default); corrected_newton_derivative = false (F-01); per-step failure semantics (F-08); /31 and 381 blend weights (F-10)\nD2 Cretaceous stage 1 runs on the time-array centres run_on_time_centers = true in experiments/cretaceous/*.toml (F-02)\nD3 Cretaceous step at 90 Ma cretaceous_age = 90.0 (F-03); selected by age, not index\nD4 temperature-only experiments may accept failed runs reject_failed_runs = false by default (F-04)\nD5 chunked Latin hypercube constant_design = :chunked_lhs (default); :global_lhs for the R design\nD6 do not store covariance draws save_covariances = false; sample_keys identify every sample\nD7 JLD2 results plus CSV summaries save_result, write_summary_csvs\nD8 single package GeocarbPrecalibration.jl; the model core does not depend on the workflow\nD9 GPL-3.0-or-later LICENSE copied from the original","title":"Decisions adopted (plan §9, proposed defaults)"},{"category":"section","location":"porting_notes/#Deliberate-differences-from-the-R/Fortran-code","page":"Porting notes","text":"Literal precision (F-05). The compiled Fortran uses single-precision values of 0.01, 0.9, 0.2, 0.7, 0.6, 0.8, 1.2 and log(2.0). Version 1 reproduces them by default (fortran_single_literals = true), so this is not a difference unless you opt out. With false, every literal is Float64; on the 1000-sample legacy comparison that changes the pass/fail outcome of 101 runs and CO2 by up to 14 % in otherwise valid steps, so treat it as a model change, not a precision improvement.\nLast-bit rounding of exp, log and ^. Julia's maths library and the C runtime gfortran links against round differently now and then. Valid steps agree to about 4e-9 relative; after a run's first failed step the legacy model keeps integrating from an invalid state (F-08) and the difference grows to about 1e-6. The tests therefore use rtol = 1e-8 before and 1e-4 after the first failure (see Testing).\nNaN instead of exceptions. Powers and logarithms of negative numbers give NaN as in Fortran (nanpow, nanlog), never a DomainError.\ngym_factor = 0 is well defined (the Fortran divides by it; F-09).\nΔT2x ramp is off unless dT2X_ramp = true. The R default supp_experiment_parameters = c(130, 80, 1, 1) silently switched it on (F-09).\nRandom numbers. Julia uses StableRNG streams per chunk, so individual samples differ from R's. Constants come from a Latin hypercube per chunk by default (D5).\nSingle chunk. n_sample ≤ n_per_chunk works (a stub in R, F-12).\nCretaceous stage 2. The forcing centres are the mean accepted series of stage 1, matched by name. In R, par_time_mean_cret.csv was written with row names and read back with read.csv, which adds an X column, so every forcing shifted by one column and Sr became 1, 2, …, 58 (F-27, found during the port). Please check whether the published stage-2 results were affected.\nSeeds. lhs_sampling_cret.R overrode the driver seed with set.seed(2021) (F-18); in Julia the seed comes only from the configuration.\nAnalysis. The duplicated deltaT2Xglac row and quantiles of placeholder zeros (F-17) are not reproduced. Hindcasts reuse saved outputs. Numbers are written to numbers.toml instead of being printed.","title":"Deliberate differences from the R/Fortran code"},{"category":"section","location":"porting_notes/#Not-ported-(yet)","page":"Porting notes","text":"Reading the legacy .RData outputs (RData.jl could be added as a package extension).\nSeven of the seventeen R figures (KDE panels of every parameter, sample-size convergence, the supplemental hindcast grid, Cretaceous panels). The numbers behind them are available from src/analysis.\nThe PR2011 and LENTON time-array files and the PR2011 prior file are not in the repository (F-14). The configuration accepts them once they are added to data/.","title":"Not ported (yet)"},{"category":"section","location":"experiments/#Experiments","page":"Experiments","text":"Experiments are TOML files in experiments/; load_experiments turns a file into one or more ExperimentConfigs.\n\nname         = \"main\"\nparam_choice = \"all\"        # → GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_all.csv\ndata_choice  = \"F2017\"      # Foster et al. (2017) CO2 proxies\nfsr_choice   = \"DT2019\"     # → GEOCARB_input_arrays_NewDegassing.csv\nn_sample     = 20_000_000   # at most this many simulations\nn_per_chunk  = 10_000\nn_accept_min = 10_000       # stop once this many are accepted\noutput_dir   = \"results/main\"\n\n[grid]                      # one experiment per (constraint, threshold)\nconstraint      = [\"co2\", \"temp\", \"both\"]\nthreshold       = [0.30, 0.35, 0.40, 0.45, 0.50]\nseed_multiplier = 1234      # seed = 1234 × experiment index, as in lhs_driver.R\n\n[model]                     # ModelOptions; empty = run_geocarb.f90\n\nOther keys: constraint, threshold and seed (single experiment), tag_suffix, reject_failed_runs, cretaceous_age, run_on_time_centers, time_center_from, constant_design, time_sampler, save_covariances, nsig, T0, explicit file names (prior_file, time_array_file, proxy_file, temperature_file, variance_reduction_file) and data_dir. Unknown keys are errors, so typos are caught.\n\nRun with\n\njulia --project=. -t auto scripts/run_experiment.jl experiments/main/grid.toml [--only tag=ct_out30] [--dry-run] [--checkpoint]\n\nEach experiment writes <output_dir>/<tag>/result.jld2 (save_result), constants.csv, prcout.csv and provenance.toml.","title":"Experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"experiments/#Adding-an-experiment","page":"Experiments","text":"Copy a TOML file and change it. A new model variant needs a new ModelOptions field (and its implementation in src/model/), plus a test. A new acceptance criterion needs a new AcceptanceRule subtype with an accepts method; the engine does not change.","title":"Adding an experiment"},{"category":"section","location":"experiments/#Running-on-a-cluster","page":"Experiments","text":"Set the number of threads with julia -t N (or JULIA_NUM_THREADS). Use --checkpoint for long jobs. To split one experiment across nodes, run with different --only filters, or give each node its own seed with [grid] seeds = [...] and combine the results with merge_results.","title":"Running on a cluster"},{"category":"section","location":"parameters/#Parameters","page":"Parameters","text":"The 56 constant parameters and 12 time-varying forcings, with their meaning and units. The table is generated from src/parameters/names.jl, the single source of truth for names.\n\nname meaning units prior bounds\nACT Activation energy for dissolution of Mg- and Ca-silicates on land (0.03 ≈ 20 kJ/mol; 0.08 ≈ 55 kJ/mol; Berner 2004 p. 28) 1/K gaussian, mean 0.09, 2σ 0.045 [0.0, Inf]\nACTcarb Activation energy for dissolution of carbonates on land 1/K gaussian, mean 0.087, 2σ 0.0435 [0.0, Inf]\nVNV Rate of chemical weathering in volcanic silicate rocks relative to non-volcanic silicate rocks - gaussian, mean 5.0, 2σ 2.5 [0.0, Inf]\nNV Coefficient relating physical erosion to the mean 87Sr/86Sr of non-volcanic silicate rocks - gaussian, mean 0.0075, 2σ 0.00375 [0.0, Inf]\nexp_NV Exponent relating physical erosion to the mean 87Sr/86Sr of non-volcanic silicate rocks - gaussian, mean 0.67, 2σ 0.335 [0.0, Inf]\nLIFE Rate of chemical weathering in a minimally vegetated world relative to the present (angiosperm-dominated) world - gaussian, mean 0.25, 2σ 0.125 [0.0, Inf]\nGYM Rate of chemical weathering by gymnosperms relative to angiosperms - gaussian, mean 0.875, 2σ 0.415625 [0.0, Inf]\nFERT Exponent for the fraction of vegetation whose growth is stimulated by CO2, in [2RCO2/(1+RCO2)]^FERT - gaussian, mean 0.4, 2σ 0.2 [0.0, 1.0]\nexp_fnBb Exponent for the effect of climate on silicate or carbonate weathering in the absence of vascular plants - gaussian, mean 0.5, 2σ 0.125 [0.0, Inf]\ndeltaT2X Climate sensitivity (warming per CO2 doubling); deltaT2X = GCM·ln(2) °C log-normal, median 3.0, factor 1.25 [1.5, Inf]\nGLAC Factor by which deltaT2X changes during times with large continental ice sheets - gaussian, mean 2.0, 2σ 1.0 [1.0, Inf]\nJ Coefficient in CAPd13C = CAPd13C_0 + J·(O2/38 − 1) per mil gaussian, mean 4.0, 2σ 2.0 [0.0, Inf]\nn Exponent in CAPd34S = CAPd34S_0·(O2/38)^n - gaussian, mean 1.0, 2σ 0.5 [0.0, Inf]\nWs Effect on temperature of the linear increase in solar luminosity over time °C gaussian, mean 7.4, 2σ 3.7 [-Inf, Inf]\nexp_fD Exponent that scales the dilution of dissolved HCO3- with runoff (fD) - gaussian, mean 0.65, 2σ 0.325 [0.0, Inf]\nFwpa_0 Sulfate flux from oxidative weathering of old pyrite at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 0.25, 2σ 0.125 [0.0, Inf]\nFwsa_0 Sulfate flux from weathering of CaSO4 sulfur at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 0.5, 2σ 0.25 [0.0, Inf]\nFwga_0 Carbon flux from weathering of old sedimentary organic matter at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 0.5, 2σ 0.25 [0.0, Inf]\nFwca_0 Carbon flux from weathering of old Ca and Mg carbonates at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 2.0, 2σ 1.0 [0.0, Inf]\nFmg_0 Carbon degassing flux from volcanism, metamorphism and diagenesis of organic matter at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 1.25, 2σ 0.625 [0.0, Inf]\nFmc_0 Carbon degassing flux from volcanism, metamorphism and diagenesis of carbonates at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 6.67, 2σ 1.50075 [0.0, Inf]\nFmp_0 Sulfur degassing flux from volcanism, metamorphism and diagenesis of pyrite at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 0.25, 2σ 0.125 [0.0, Inf]\nFms_0 Sulfur degassing flux from volcanism, metamorphism and diagenesis of CaSO4 sulfur at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 0.5, 2σ 0.25 [0.0, Inf]\nFwsi_0 Weathering flux for all Ca and Mg silicates at present 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 6.67, 2σ 0.83375 [0.0, Inf]\nXvolc_0 Fraction of total Ca and Mg silicate weathering derived from volcanic rocks at present - gaussian, mean 0.35, 2σ 0.07875 [0.0, 1.0]\nCAPd13C_0 Carbon isotopic fractionation between shallow-marine carbonate and organic matter at present per mil gaussian, mean 27.0, 2σ 1.89 [0.0, Inf]\nCAPd34S_0 Sulfur isotopic fractionation between shallow-marine CaSO4 sulfur and pyrite sulfur at present per mil gaussian, mean 35.0, 2σ 4.7 [0.0, Inf]\noxygen_570 Mass of atmospheric O2 at 570 Ma (7.5 and 143 correspond to 5% and 50% O2) 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 25.0, 2σ 12.5 [7.5, 143.0]\nGy_570 Mass of young crustal organic carbon at 570 Ma 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 250.0, 2σ 62.5 [0.0, Inf]\nCy_570 Mass of young crustal carbonate carbon at 570 Ma 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 1000.0, 2σ 475.0 [0.0, Inf]\nCa_570 Mass of old crustal carbonate carbon at 570 Ma 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 4000.0, 2σ 1300.0 [0.0, Inf]\nSsy_570 Mass of young CaSO4 sulfur at 570 Ma 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 150.0, 2σ 56.25 [0.0, Inf]\nSpy_570 Mass of young pyrite sulfur at 570 Ma 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 20.0, 2σ 10.0 [0.0, Inf]\ndlsy_570 d34S of young CaSO4 sulfur at 570 Ma per mil gaussian, mean 35.0, 2σ 4.7 [-Inf, Inf]\ndlcy_570 d13C of young carbonate carbon at 570 Ma per mil gaussian, mean 3.0, 2σ 2.1 [-Inf, Inf]\ndlpy_570 d34S of young pyrite sulfur at 570 Ma per mil gaussian, mean -10.0, 2σ 4.7 [-Inf, Inf]\ndlpa_570 d34S of old pyrite sulfur at 570 Ma per mil gaussian, mean -10.0, 2σ 4.7 [-Inf, Inf]\ndlgy_570 d13C of young organic matter at 570 Ma per mil gaussian, mean -23.5, 2σ 2.1 [-Inf, Inf]\ndlga_570 d13C of old organic matter at 570 Ma per mil gaussian, mean -23.5, 2σ 2.1 [-Inf, Inf]\nRcy_570 87Sr/86Sr of young carbonates undergoing weathering at 570 Ma - gaussian, mean 0.7095, 2σ 0.0021 [-Inf, Inf]\nRca_570 87Sr/86Sr of old carbonates undergoing weathering at 570 Ma - gaussian, mean 0.709, 2σ 0.0021 [-Inf, Inf]\nRv_570 87Sr/86Sr of sub-aerial and submarine volcanic rocks at 570 Ma - gaussian, mean 0.704, 2σ 0.001365 [-Inf, Inf]\nRg_570 87Sr/86Sr of non-volcanic silicates at 570 Ma - gaussian, mean 0.722, 2σ 0.0021 [-Inf, Inf]\nFob Ca and Mg flux between basalt and seawater 10^18 mol/Myr gaussian, mean 4.0, 2σ 2.0 [0.0, Inf]\nCOC Mass of carbon in the ocean 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 2.0, 2σ 1.0 [0.0, Inf]\nGa Mass of old crustal organic carbon 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 1000.0, 2σ 75.0 [0.0, Inf]\nSsa Mass of old CaSO4 sulfur 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 150.0, 2σ 75.0 [0.0, Inf]\nSpa Mass of old pyrite sulfur 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 280.0, 2σ 140.0 [0.0, Inf]\nST Mass of sulfur in oceans + interacting rocks 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 600.0, 2σ 300.0 [0.0, Inf]\ndlst d34S of ST per mil gaussian, mean 4.0, 2σ 3.055 [-Inf, Inf]\nCT Mass of carbon in oceans + interacting rocks 10^18 mol gaussian, mean 6252.0, 2σ 468.9 [0.0, Inf]\ndlct d13C of CT per mil gaussian, mean -3.5, 2σ 0.252 [-Inf, Inf]\nkwpy Rate constant expressing mass dependence for young pyrite sulfur weathering 1/Myr gaussian, mean 0.01, 2σ 0.005 [0.0, Inf]\nkwsy Rate constant expressing mass dependence for young CaSO4 sulfur weathering 1/Myr gaussian, mean 0.01, 2σ 0.005 [0.0, Inf]\nkwgy Rate constant expressing mass dependence for young organic matter weathering 1/Myr gaussian, mean 0.018, 2σ 0.009 [0.0, Inf]\nkwcy Rate constant expressing mass dependence for young carbonate weathering 1/Myr gaussian, mean 0.018, 2σ 0.009 [0.0, Inf]\n\nforcing meaning units\nSr Strontium isotopic composition of shallow-marine carbonate, (87Sr/86Sr − 0.7)·10^4 -\nd13C Carbon isotopic composition of shallow-marine carbonate (DLCOC in the BASIC code) per mil\nd34S Sulfur isotopic composition of shallow-marine sulfate (DLSOC in the BASIC code) per mil\nfR Effect of relief on chemical weathering relative to the present -\nfL Land area covered by carbonates relative to the present -\nfA Land area relative to the present -\nfAw_fA Fraction of land area undergoing chemical weathering (normalised to the present) -\nfD Global river runoff relative to the present, without luminosity and CO2 effects -\nGEOG Change in mean land surface temperature from palaeogeography alone (Berner 2004 p. 35) °C\nRT Coefficient of continental runoff versus temperature change, fD = 1 + RT·(T − T0) 1/°C\nfSR Seafloor creation (degassing) rate relative to the present -\nfC Effect of carbonate content of subducting crust on CO2 degassing relative to the present -\n\n","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"precalibration/#Precalibration","page":"Precalibration","text":"Precalibration keeps the parameter samples whose simulations pass through observational windows often enough (Mills et al. 2019; Wong et al. 2021).\n\nConstants. A Latin hypercube in 56 dimensions is mapped through the truncated priors (TruncatedPrior). With constant_design = :chunked_lhs (the default) every chunk is its own Latin hypercube. With :global_lhs a single design covers all samples, as in the R code. That uses about 9 GB at 20 million samples (finding F-11), and because the run usually stops early, only the first chunks of that design are ever evaluated.\nForcings. Each of the 12 series is drawn around its centre (InverseWishartSampler): Σ ~ IW(ν, (ν−p−1)·diag(σ²)), then x ~ N(μ, Σ), with ν from inverse_wishart_dof. fR, fL, fA, fAw_fA, fC are then divided by their present-day value and GEOG has it subtracted. MultivariateTSampler draws from the same distribution (its exact multivariate-t marginal) in O(p) instead of O(p³).\nWindows. CO2: per 10 Myr step, the envelope of log10(co2) ± nsig·log10(co2_high/co2) over the proxies in that step (co2_windows). Temperature: Mills et al. (2019) mean ± nsig·(max/min − mean) (temperature_windows).\n% outbound. The fraction of steps outside the window; 1 if any step failed (CO2 = Inf) (legacy_percent_outbound).\nAcceptance. OutboundRules for CO2 and/or temperature, optionally a CretaceousWindowRule, combined with AllOf.\n\nThe engine (run_precalibration) processes chunks in parallel. Each chunk has its own StableRNG seeded from (seed, chunk) and results are consumed in chunk order, so a run gives the same samples on 1 or 64 threads and on any Julia version. With checkpoint_dir, finished chunks are saved and reused after an interruption.","title":"Precalibration"},{"category":"section","location":"#GeocarbPrecalibration.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia implementation of the GEOCARBSULFvolc long-term carbon-cycle model and of the precalibration workflow used by Wong et al. (2021), A tighter constraint on Earth-system sensitivity from long-term temperature and carbon-cycle observations. It is a port of the R/Fortran code GEOCARB-precalibration by Tony Wong and Ying Cui.","title":"GeocarbPrecalibration.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Statement-of-need","page":"Home","text":"GEOCARB-type models are the standard tool for reconstructing Phanerozoic CO2 and for constraining Earth-system sensitivity from the geological record. The original precalibration code mixes R driver scripts with a compiled Fortran model, edits experiment settings in place, and is difficult to extend, test or run in parallel. GeocarbPrecalibration.jl gives the same model and workflow as one documented, tested Julia package. It reproduces the compiled Fortran to rounding error, which is checked continuously against the original code. Runs are deterministic across thread counts, experiments are declared in TOML files, and model variants are options rather than copies of the source.","title":"Statement of need"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"julia> using GeocarbPrecalibration\n\njulia> table = read_prior_table(datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_summaries_calib_all.csv\"));\n\njulia> arrays = read_time_arrays(datapath(\"GEOCARB_input_arrays_NewDegassing.csv\"));\n\njulia> p = mean_constants(table);   # the 56 constants at their prior means\n\njulia> f = arrays.center;           # the 12 forcings at their central estimates\n\njulia> r = run_geocarb(p, f);       # 570 Ma → present, 10 Myr steps\n\njulia> succeeded(r)                 # every step converged\ntrue\n\njulia> round(r.co2[end]; digits = 1)            # present-day CO2, ppmv (Inf on failed steps)\n243.7\n\njulia> round(temperature(r)[end]; digits = 2)   # °C, comparable with the Mills et al. (2019) windows\n14.78\n\nRun a (small) precalibration experiment from a configuration file. This takes about a minute, so it is not run as a doctest:\n\ncfgs = load_experiments(\"experiments/example_small.toml\")\nres = run_precalibration(only(cfgs))\nclimate_sensitivity_summary(res)\n\nor from the command line, using all CPU threads:\n\njulia --project=. -t auto scripts/run_experiment.jl experiments/main/grid.toml --only tag=ct_out30","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reproducing-the-paper","page":"Home","text":"step R (original) Julia\nmain experiments source(\"lhs_driver.R\") scripts/run_experiment.jl experiments/main/grid.toml and …/ct_out30_extra_seeds.toml\nsupplemental experiments source(\"lhs_driver_supp.R\") scripts/run_experiment.jl experiments/supplemental/<name>.toml\nCretaceous experiment lhs_driver_cret.R, edit, cretaceous_experiment.R, edit, rerun experiments/cretaceous/stage1.toml, then stage2.toml\nanalysis and figures analysis_and_plots.R scripts/analysis_main.jl, analysis_supplemental.jl, analysis_cretaceous.jl (figures with CairoMakie)\n\nThe random number generators of R and Julia differ, so individual samples differ; the model itself reproduces the compiled Fortran to rounding error (see Testing).","title":"Reproducing the paper"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-to-cite","page":"Home","text":"Please cite Wong et al. (2021) and this software (see CITATION.cff). The code is licensed under the GNU General Public License v3 or later, like the original.","title":"How to cite"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#Testing","page":"Testing","text":"julia --project=. -e \"using Pkg; Pkg.test()\"                          # everything except the R comparison\nGROUP=unit julia --project=. -e \"using Pkg; Pkg.test()\"               # one group\njulia --project=. test/legacy_comparison/run_comparison.jl            # compare with the original R/Fortran\n\ngroup what\nunit physics pieces (climate, vegetation, fluxes, solver), step-status invariants on 200 prior draws, supplemental options, input validation, zero allocations, Float32 and ForwardDiff inputs\nio CSV reader (BOM, CRLF, CR-only, quotes), prior table, time arrays, proxies, temperature data\nsampling Latin hypercube strata, truncated priors (KS tests), inverse-Wishart degrees of freedom, sampler moments, IW ≡ multivariate-t\nconstraints CO2 and temperature windows, % outbound semantics\nworkflow configuration files, engine (stopping rule, single chunk, determinism across thread counts, checkpoint/resume), save/load, analysis\nregression golden master against fixtures from the compiled Fortran (when generated; see test/fixtures/README.md)\nquality Aqua.jl (stale dependencies, compat bounds, piracy, ambiguities)\ndoctests the jldoctest examples in docstrings (the manual's doctests run in docs/make.jl)\nlegacy end-to-end comparison with the original R/Fortran code; needs R (see test/legacy_comparison/README.md)\n\nThe legacy comparison runs the unmodified R code on 1000 samples and feeds exactly those samples to Julia, using the default fortran_single_literals = true. The regression golden test does the same on 100 of those samples stored in test/fixtures/fortran/, so it runs without R.","title":"Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#Tolerances","page":"Testing","text":"Both tests judge every run in two regions, split at the run's first failed step (the first Inf in the legacy CO2):\n\ncheck requirement\nInf/NaN positions identical (the same steps fail)\n% outbound identical\nvalues before the first failed step abs(a − b) ≤ 1e-10 + 1e-8·max(abs(a), abs(b))\nvalues at or after the first failed step abs(a − b) ≤ 1e-10 + 1e-4·max(abs(a), abs(b))\n\nWhy two regions: Julia's exp, log and ^ and the C runtime used by gfortran occasionally round the last bit differently. Two builds of the same Fortran (the main and supplemental libraries) already differ by up to 8e-10 for that reason. In a valid step the model is well conditioned and the difference stays below about 4e-9. After a failed step the legacy code keeps integrating from an invalid state (finding F-08), the next steps are badly conditioned, and the same last-bit difference grows to about 1e-6. A real porting error is still caught: it changes valid steps far more than 1e-8, or moves a failure, or changes a % outbound.\n\nThe report (test/legacy_comparison/output/comparison_report.txt) lists the worst cells of each region with their run, age, step status, solver iterations and the age of the run's first failure. The tolerances are kept in test/tolerances.toml, which the golden-master test reads too; CI never overrides them, and a change to the file needs a CHANGELOG entry that says so. For experiments they can be overridden with --rtol-clean, --rtol-post and --atol. With CairoMakie installed, the run also draws figures of these differences into test/legacy_comparison/output/figures/ (see test/legacy_comparison/README.md).\n\nThe comparison also reports, for information only, how far Julia with fortran_single_literals = false is from the legacy output (pass/fail flips and relative differences).","title":"Tolerances"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#Continuous-integration","page":"Testing","text":"Before code reaches GitHub, the prek hooks in .pre-commit-config.yaml (see CONTRIBUTING.md) format it, and on every git push run the CHANGELOG/AI-usage check, the golden test and the full live comparison with the R/Fortran code.\n\nGitHub Actions (.github/workflows/) runs on every push and pull request:\n\nworkflow check name what\nCI.yml CI result Pkg.test() on Linux, macOS and Windows × Julia 1.10 (LTS) and 1, plus 4 threads and the pre-release (allowed to fail); coverage to Codecov. Includes the regression golden test, so every platform is checked against the Fortran output\nLegacy.yml Legacy comparison on a pinned toolchain (ubuntu-24.04, R 4.6.1, dated CRAN snapshot): builds the original Fortran with gfortran, runs the unmodified R code and run_comparison.jl, then regenerates the golden fixtures from that Fortran and runs the golden test on them; report in the job summary, all output as an artifact\nDocs.yml Documentation docstring doctests, then docs/make.jl (manual doctests, strict cross-references), deployed to GitHub Pages from main (dev), tags (stable) and pull requests (previews)\nFormat.yml Formatting BlueStyle, with the JuliaFormatter 2.x pinned in tools/format (the same as the pre-commit hook)\nChanges.yml Changelog and AI usage pull requests: CHANGELOG entry, AI disclosure and trailers, special changes named (scripts/ci/check_changes.jl)\nLint.yml Lint the pre-commit hooks on all files: file hygiene, actionlint, zizmor\nMetadata.yml Metadata CITATION.cff validity, presence of the AI usage disclosure, matching versions\nPaper.yml  JOSS paper PDF (when paper/ changes)\nWeekly.yml  Mondays: tests with the newest dependencies, legacy comparison with the newest R/CRAN/runner, documentation link check, JET\nRelease.yml  tag vX.Y.Z: checks versions, CHANGELOG and placeholders, publishes a GitHub release (archived by Zenodo)\n\nBranch protection on main should require CI result, Legacy comparison, Documentation, Formatting, Metadata, Changelog and AI usage and Lint.","title":"Continuous integration"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#Retiring-the-comparison-with-the-R/Fortran-code","page":"Testing","text":"The live comparison is meant to be temporary. To drop it:\n\ndelete .github/workflows/Legacy.yml, the legacy job in Weekly.yml and the legacy-comparison hook in .pre-commit-config.yaml;\nremove Legacy comparison from the required checks of main;\nkeep test/regression, test/fixtures/fortran/ and test/tolerances.toml. They keep pinning the model to the original Fortran output on every platform, without R. legacy/ and test/legacy_comparison/ can stay so the comparison can still be run by hand.","title":"Retiring the comparison with the R/Fortran code"}]
}
